WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.113
- I've only got one life
motto, run when chased.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.600
Don't order fish at a steakhouse.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.500
Undertakers and chauffeurs
are the only people

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.000
who should be allowed by
law to wear black suits.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
White shirt plus tan
face equals confidence.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.170
Those are just a few of the rules

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.280
Roger Stone lives his life by.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.270
Hell, he's written a
book about those rules

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.510
titled, quote, Stone's Rules,

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.220
How to Win at Politics,
Business, and Style.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.210
Now, at the moment,
Stone is less well-known

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.850
for his thoughts on double-breasted suits,

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.210
his thoughts are, quote, never wear

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.120
a double breasted suit and a
button-down collar end quote,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.020
then he is for his arrest
and his indictment on charges

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.300
that he lied to Congress
and tampered with witnesses

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.330
regarding what he knew when
about emails stolen by Russia

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.210
during the 2016 campaign and subsequently

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.400
posted on the WikiLeaks website.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.040
Whew, alright, let me start
from the beginning-ish.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.120
Roger Stone's first brush with politics

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.610
came in 1960 when we was in first grade.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.480
He supported John F. Kennedy in that race

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.110
because, in Stone's words, he
had better hair than Nixon.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.330
Fact check, that is true.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.290
Stone told his classmates,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.400
remember we're talking about
first grade here people,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.260
that if Nixon won, he'd make them

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.560
all go to school on Saturdays.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.400
Ah!

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.680
In the class's mock
election, Kennedy won easily,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.060
a victory that Stone took credit for.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.090
Kennedy swept the election, he once said,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.760
because of disinformation
I spread about Nixon.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.600
It was a kind of first experience.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.080
A decade later, though,
Stone was actually working

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.700
with Nixon's 1972 reelection
campaign as a junior scheduler,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.900
and because he is Roger Stone,

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.900
he was doing a lot more
than just scheduling.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.610
Stone adopted a pseudonym,
does that sound like

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.030
anyone else you know, and made a donation

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.810
in the name of the Young
Socialists Alliance

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.130
to Pete McCloskey, the
California congressman

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.300
primarying Nixon an antiwar message.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.640
Then Stone sent proof of his donation

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.240
to a New Hampshire newspaper as evidence

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.783
that McCloskey was in
league with the socialists.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.160
It was at the end of that decade

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.030
that the paths of Stone
and Donald Trump crossed.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.010
Stone was running a trio
of northeastern states

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.760
for Ronald Reagan's 1980
presidential bid when he scored

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.230
a meeting with longtime Trump
mentor, Roy Cohn in 1979.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.450
Cohn told Stone he had to meet Trump.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.830
They met, and Trump promised support

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.460
for Reagan, sending Stone to his father.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.990
Fred Trump promised Stone
$200,000 in donations,

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:37.320
and soon checks for $1,000 each,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.820
the maximum donation under
the law at that point,

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.873
began pouring into Reagan's headquarters.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.640
Stone spent the 80's on
Reagan's campaign team,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.350
and then in 1988 served

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.460
as a senior consultant
to then Pice president

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.600
George H.W. Bush's presidential campaign.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.100
During that time, he also
started a political consulting

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.320
and lobbying business with
the Republican campaign

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.320
Lee Atwater and, wait
for it, Paul Manafort.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.150
By 1996, Stone was back
on the campaign trail

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.740
as a senior advisor to Bob
Dole's presidential bid.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.360
Now that gig ended when
the National Enquirer,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.610
everything old is new again,
published a story head line

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.800
Top Dole Aide Caught in Group Sex Ring.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.500
The piece alleged that Stone had ads

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.223
in a magazine called Local Swing Fever.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.940
Stone called the whole thing a set up

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.160
but resigned from the
Dole campaign anyway.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.430
That campaign largely
ended Stone's relevance

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.900
as a mainstream Republican campaign guru.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.780
Which dovetailed nicely with Stone's

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.150
increasing role in Trumps orbit.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.820
In 1999, Stone tried to gin up excitement

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.760
for the idea of Trump running
as the Reform party nominee

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.280
for president but trump decided against it

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.480
and seemingly against Stone.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.800
Roger is a stone cold loser.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.130
Trump told the New Yorker.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.830
He always tries taking credit
for things he never did.

00:03:56.686 --> 00:03:59.180
Hmm, and yet, Trump couldn't quit Stone,

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.680
allowing the self-proclaimed
dirty trickster

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.900
to remain in his orbit

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.850
as the billionaire business
man got more and more serious

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.440
about running for office.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.510
A quick look at Stone's rules shows why.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.330
The two men shared a similar
view on how to succeed

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.430
in life and in politics.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:18.140
Considered these Stone's Rules.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.180
Attack, attack, attack never defend.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.730
Nothing is on the level.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.350
Lay low, play dumb, keep moving.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.200
And finally and most
importantly quoting Nixon,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.240
the only thing worse in politics

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.440
than being wrong is being boring.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.810
Now given that sort of mind
meld, it's not surprising

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.500
that when Trump formally
entered the presidential race,

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.680
Stone was installed as the
campaigns top strategist.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.820
It was also not at all surprising

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.410
that less than two months
later, Stone was out.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.990
Trump said that he had fired Stone.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.340
Stone said that he quit

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.560
because of course that's
what they both said.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.640
Stone still didn't leave
Trump's orbit though

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.840
and in the summer of
2016, he allegedly was

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.530
tasked by a senior
Trump campaign official,

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.410
we still don't know who
that is, with reaching out

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.840
to Wikileaks and seeing
if he could learn anything

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.670
about the groups plans
to post those hack emails

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.570
from the democratic national committee

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:20.140
and Hilary Clinton campaign
chairman, John Podesta.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.330
Sidebar, we now know
those emails were stolen

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.500
by the Russians as part of their effort

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.250
to sway the election for Donald Trump.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.700
Now according to the indictment
from special counsel,

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.100
Robert Mueller's office, Stone
repeatedly lied to congress

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.840
about his ties to Wikileaks

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.050
and tried to get other
witnesses to do the same.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:43.450
Stone has pleaded not
guilty to the charges

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:45.120
and the White House has said quote

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.740
this has nothing to do with
the President, end quote.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:53.810
Maybe but Stones history suggests
there's reason to believe

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.470
the Mueller indictment is a lot more fact

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.100
than it is fiction.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.700
How will Stone and Trump
handle whatever comes

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.200
next from Mueller?

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.500
Stone's Rules, again, come to the rescue.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:11.210
Admit nothing, deny everything,
launch counterattack.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.513
And that is the point.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.070
We make new Point episodes every week.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.090
Check back every Tuesday
and every Thursday

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.523
for the latest and greatest
in political videos.

